<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joaquín Balaguer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joaquín <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> was a Dominican politician and writer who served as president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> three times: in 1960–1962, from 1966 to 1978 and from 1986 to 1996. A former aide of the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a>, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a> in 1966 and ruled for twelve years with repression of the opposition and public building. His later terms were marked by disputed elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joaquin-balaguer.jpg" alt="Joaquín Balaguer" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joaquin_Balaguer_1977.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joaquín <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> was born on 1 September 1906 in Navarrete, later Villa Bisonó, in Santiago Province. He was the only son in a family of several daughters and wrote verses that were published in local magazines when he was very young. He earned a law degree at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and briefly studied at the Sorbonne.</p><p>His political career began in 1930 with an appointment as Attorney in the Court of Properties, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a> took control of the government. Under Trujillo he served as secretary of education from 1949 to 1955 and as secretary of foreign relations. In 1957 Trujillo had his brother Héctor re-elected president and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> as vice president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1960 pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> persuaded Trujillo that it was inappropriate to have a relative as president, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> succeeded Héctor. After Trujillo's assassination in May 1961, real power lay with his son Ramfis. A Council of State led by Balaguer was formed in January 1962, but riots followed, and he resigned on 16 January and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>.</p><p>After the Dominican Civil War of 1965, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops intervened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> returned with American backing and founded the Reformist Party. In 1966 he easily defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a>. His twelve years in office saw schools, hospitals, dams and roads built and steady economic growth, and he won re-election in 1970 and 1974. His rule had an authoritarian character: opponents were jailed and sometimes killed. According to Luisa de Peña Díaz, director of the Memorial Museum of Dominican Resistance, 11,000 people were tortured, imprisoned, disappeared or killed between 1966 and 1978.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1978 he lost to Antonio Guzmán of the Dominican Revolutionary Party, and he lost again in 1982. He won the presidency in 1986, at 80 and almost blind, and his third presidency was more tolerant of opposition parties and human rights. His narrow victories over Bosch in 1990 and over José Francisco Peña Gómez in 1994 were met with charges of fraud, and in 1994 an investigation found that about 200,000 people had been removed from the voting rolls. He agreed to hold new elections in 1996, in which he did not run. He died of heart failure on 14 July 2002, aged 95.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joaquín Balaguer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joaquín Balaguer</b>, a former aide of Trujillo, defeated Juan Bosch and introduced a new constitution in November 1966.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bisonó, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.56,-70.87&amp;year=1968" aria-label="Bisonó on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joaquín Balaguer"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4129 5846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4129 5846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.56,-70.87&amp;year=1968">What was here in 1968? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
